statement:
Let n, l >= 0, mu a measure on R^(n+l), H : R^(n+l) -> R^(n+l), w : R^(n+l) -> R, y in R^(n+l), phi : R -> R, I, a : R -> R, and reals S, epsilon, M, beta with epsilon > 0 and beta < 1. Write r = |x - y|. Assume:
- mu(B_S(y)) < infinity;
- w is mu-a.e. strongly measurable with 0 <= w <= 1 mu-a.e.;
- H is mu-integrable on B_S(y);
- phi is C^1 and nonincreasing, with phi(t) = 1 for t <= 1 and phi(t) = 0 for t >= 1 + epsilon;
- (R_rho) for every rho > 0 with (1 + epsilon) rho <= S: integral [n phi(r/rho) + (r/rho) phi'(r/rho)(1 - w)] dmu = - integral phi(r/rho)(x - y).H dmu;
- I(t) = integral phi(r/t) dmu and a(t) = t^(-(n+1)) (integral phi(r/t)(x - y).H dmu)/(1 + t^(-n) I(t)) for every real t;
- |a(t)| <= M t^(-beta) whenever t > 0 and (1 + epsilon) t < S.
Let 0 < sigma <= rho with (1 + epsilon) rho < S. Put F(t) := exp(-integral_0^t a), c := integral_0^rho |a| and A := integral (phi(r/rho) - phi(r/sigma)) w/r^n dmu, with a/0 = 0. Then e^(-c) A <= F(rho)(1 + rho^(-n) I(rho)) - F(sigma)(1 + sigma^(-n) I(sigma)) <= e^c (1 + epsilon)^n A.
